<p style="text-align: center; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Today Is Tuesday! – Lesson # 3</p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-size: 16px; color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Tuesday!</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Use basic words or phrases to identify time words. Interactive: Exchange simple questions and answers about the current day.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify specific time-related words, such as days of the week and the weekend, in simple sentences (e.g., "Saturday and Sunday are the weekends." "Today is Tuesday."). Can exchange simple questions and answers about the current day, using basic time-related words (e.g., "What day is it today?" "Today is Monday."). Can respond to questions about time using basic words and phrases to indicate days (e.g., "What is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Wednesday." or "It's the weekend.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by leading the "Days of the Week" song with interactive actions (e.g., clapping for Monday, jumping for Tuesday, etc.). Students sing along and perform the actions, reactivating the vocabulary introduced in previous lessons.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large flashcard for "Tuesday" and says clearly, "Today is Tuesday. Today is Tuesday." The teacher models the pronunciation of /w/ in "weekend" by exaggerating the sound, and /d/ in "day" by repeating words like "day" and "today." The teacher then points to a calendar and asks, "What day is today?" modeling the full question.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to Monday on a calendar: "Is today Monday?" (Students respond: "No.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to Tuesday: "Show me Tuesday." (Students point to Tuesday on their mini-calendars or flashcards).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful illustrated weekly schedule chart displaying simple activities for each day (e.g., Monday: school, Tuesday: library, Saturday: park, Sunday: home/relaxing). Each day is clearly labeled with its name. The chart will visually reinforce the theme "Today Is Tuesday!" and the scenario "All Week Long!" through daily routines.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Exchanging questions and answers about the current day using simple phrases like "Today is Monday." and "What day is today?" Using time-related vocabulary: identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "Tuesday" on the chart: "What day is this?" (Choral response: "Tuesday!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to Saturday and Sunday on the chart: "Are these school days or the weekend?" (Choral response: "The weekend!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to an activity on Tuesday: "What do we do on Tuesday?" (Students respond with 1-2 words from the picture, e.g., "Library!").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Noun + verb + noun (e.g., "Today is Monday."), Present simple (e.g., "It is the weekend."), WH-questions (e.g., "What day is today?").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day, week, be (am, is, are), today, tomorrow, relaxing, exciting, busy, quiet.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Emphasizing /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic: Exchanging questions and answers about the current day.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Listen and Repeat the Day":</b> The teacher holds up individual day flashcards one by one. Students first listen, then repeat the day chorally. Then, the teacher says, "Today is..." and points to a flashcard. Students complete the sentence chorally (e.g., Teacher: "Today is...", points to Tuesday; Students: "Tuesday!").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Drill: "Day Chain":</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "Today is Tuesday." and points to a student. That student then says, "Tomorrow is Wednesday." and points to the next student. The next student says, "What day is tomorrow?" and the first student answers, "Tomorrow is Wednesday." The chain continues, varying who asks and who answers, encouraging full sentence responses.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Ask Your Friend":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of day flashcards (or a mini-calendar). Student A picks a card and asks Student B, "What day is today?" Student B responds, "Today is [day on card]." Then Student B asks, "What day is tomorrow?" and Student A responds. They swap roles and repeat with different cards. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation.</li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Pair Interview / Role-Play.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in pairs to conduct a simple interview about the days of the week, using the learned vocabulary and grammatical structures. Each pair will have a set of flashcards depicting different activities associated with days (e.g., 'playing' for Saturday, 'reading' for Wednesday, 'school' for Monday) and day names. One student acts as the interviewer, the other as the interviewee, then they switch roles.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher models the interaction first with a volunteer student. "Hello! What day is today?" "Today is Tuesday." "What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Wednesday." "Is it the weekend?" "No, it's not the weekend." "Is Saturday a relaxing day?" "Yes, it is."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then greet their partner and use their flashcards as prompts to ask: "What day is today?" "Today is [day]." "What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is [day]." "Is it the weekend?" "Yes, it is the weekend." or "No, it is not the weekend." They can also point to an activity card and ask, "Is [activity] exciting?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages polite expressions learned (e.g., "Hello," "Thank you") and monitors for fluent, albeit basic, communication, focusing on the use of 'today', 'tomorrow', 'weekend', and the days of the week.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Daily Routine Chat" (Performance stage).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the pair interview, asking and answering questions about the days of the week.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day" clearly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use "today," "tomorrow," "weekend," and at least 3-4 days of the week.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy:</b> Students form simple sentences (e.g., "Today is Monday.", "It is the weekend.") and answer WH-questions ("What day is today?") appropriately.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students engage in the conversation with their partner, using polite classroom expressions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two prompts. They draw a happy face or a sad face next to each one.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say the days of the week. (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask "What day is today?" (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to say today? (e.g., "Monday," "today")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult to say today? (e.g., "Wednesday," "tomorrow")</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The pair work proved effective for encouraging speaking at a Pre-A1.3 level. Students demonstrated good recall of the days of the week, but some still confused "today" and "tomorrow." The pronunciation of /w/ and /d/ needs continued reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will ensure more visual aids for "tomorrow" and "yesterday" if introduced, and maybe a flashcard game for quick identification of the days. Adjustments needed include having clearer sentence starters written on the board for the "Performance" stage for those struggling with sentence formation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson established oral fluency with days of the week and simple time words. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build upon this by having students write simple sentences about days and activities, such as "Today is Tuesday. It is school day."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>